🎯 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

“Your application is running, but response time has suddenly increased. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I would not immediately restart the application.

First, I want to understand where the latency is coming from.

I would check whether the issue is affecting:

• One user or many users?
• One API or the entire application?
• One server or multiple servers?
• The application or an external dependency?

I would first test the endpoint:

curl -w "\nTime: %{time_total}s\n" -o /dev/null -s https://example.com/api

Then I would check the server resources:

top
free -m
df -h

I would also check network connectivity and latency:

ping <server>

and, where appropriate:

curl -v https://example.com

Next, I would check the application logs for errors, timeouts, slow requests, or connection failures.

If the application depends on a database, API, cache, or another service, I would investigate those dependencies as well.

I would ask:

• Did traffic suddenly increase?
• Was there a recent deployment?
• Are database queries becoming slow?
• Are connections getting exhausted?
• Is CPU or memory under pressure?
• Is there network latency?
• Are external APIs responding slowly?

If monitoring tools are available, I would compare the current metrics with the normal baseline.

Once I identify the bottleneck, I would follow the approved recovery procedure.

After the fix, I would test the endpoint again and monitor response time to confirm that the issue is actually resolved.

Finally:

Issue → Investigation → Root Cause → Resolution → Preventive Action

💡 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗧𝗮𝗸𝗲𝗮𝘄𝗮𝘆

When an application is slow, don't simply say:

“The server is slow.”

Find the bottleneck.

𝗖𝗹𝗶𝗲𝗻𝘁 → 𝗡𝗲𝘁𝘄𝗼𝗿𝗸 → 𝗟𝗼𝗮𝗱 𝗕𝗮𝗹𝗮𝗻𝗰𝗲𝗿 → 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗹𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 → 𝗗𝗮𝘁𝗮𝗯𝗮𝘀𝗲/𝗗𝗲𝗽𝗲𝗻𝗱𝗲𝗻𝗰𝗶𝗲𝘀

That is the troubleshooting mindset expected from a DevOps Engineer. 🚀
